# Portfolio Tracker — Documentation

Final project, Kodilla Java Developer bootcamp.

The project consists of two applications, each in its own repository:

- **portfolio-tracker-backend** — the REST API (this repository)
- **portfolio-tracker-frontend** — the Vaadin user interface

They share no code. They communicate only over HTTP, through the REST API
described below.

---

## 1. What the project is for

**Portfolio Tracker keeps track of a cryptocurrency investment portfolio and
tells you what it is worth right now, in your own currency.**

Anyone holding crypto faces the same small, recurring annoyance: the coins were
bought at different times and different prices, exchanges report values in US
dollars, and the actual question — *am I up or down, in the money I actually
spend?* — takes a spreadsheet to answer. This application answers it directly.

### What a user can do

**Record what they bought and sold.** Every purchase and sale is entered as a
transaction, with quantity, unit price, fee and date. This ledger is the only
thing the user maintains by hand.

**See their current position.** The application works out, for each asset:

- how much is still held, after accounting for any sales;
- what it cost on average, using the average-cost method;
- what it is worth at today's price;
- the profit or loss, in absolute terms and as a percentage.

Holdings are never stored. They are recalculated from the transaction ledger
every time, so the ledger cannot drift out of step with the totals. Correcting a
mistyped transaction immediately corrects every figure derived from it.

**See the value in their own currency.** A portfolio has a reporting currency.
Prices arrive in US dollars, so the application converts them — to Polish złoty
directly, or to any other currency as a cross rate calculated through złoty.

**Set alerts.** A user can ask to be told when an asset's price rises above or
falls below a chosen figure, or when a whole portfolio's value crosses one. The
application checks these automatically every fifteen minutes. When one fires, it
is recorded and shown on the dashboard until acknowledged.

> The application is a **tracker, not an advisor**. It never suggests what to buy
> or sell. Alerts are thresholds the user sets for themselves, and the
> application only reports that a number was crossed.

**Review the history.** Every change — every transaction, every alert, every
price refresh — is written to an audit trail that can be browsed through the API.

### Why two data sources

The interesting part of this project is that neither external source is useful
alone:

- **CoinGecko** knows what a bitcoin is worth, but only in dollars.
- **NBP**, the Polish National Bank, knows what a dollar is worth in złoty, but
  nothing about crypto.

Combining them answers the question the user actually has. A portfolio holding
0.35 BTC is worth $22,395.80 according to CoinGecko; at NBP's rate of 3.7962 złoty
to the dollar, that is 85,018.94 złoty — which is the number that means something
to someone whose salary and rent are in złoty.

---

## 2. Running the project, step by step

### What you need first

- **Java 17 or newer.** Check with `java -version`.
- **An internet connection.** The applications call CoinGecko and NBP, and
  Vaadin downloads its frontend toolchain the first time it builds.
- **No database.** The backend runs an embedded H2 database that needs no setup.
  (MySQL is supported if you prefer — see section 3.)

Everything else, including Gradle, comes with the projects.

### Step 1 — Get both repositories

```
git clone <url-of-portfolio-tracker-backend>
git clone <url-of-portfolio-tracker-frontend>
```

Put them side by side in the same folder.

### Step 2 — Start the backend

In the `portfolio-tracker-backend` folder:

```
./gradlew bootRun
```

On Windows use `gradlew bootRun`.

The first run downloads dependencies and takes a minute or two. Wait for:

```
Started PortfolioTrackerApplication in 22.5 seconds
Seeded demo data: user 'demo' (id=1) with 2 portfolios, 4 assets, 6 transactions
```

The API is now at **http://localhost:8080**, and there is already demo data to
look at, so nothing starts out empty.

**If you see a "port already in use" error**, something else is using port 8080 —
Apache, XAMPP and Jenkins all default to it. Start on another port instead:

```
./gradlew bootRun --args='--server.port=8090'
```

Remember which port you chose; step 3 needs it.

### Step 3 — Start the frontend

Leave the backend running. Open a **second terminal**, go to
`portfolio-tracker-frontend`, and run:

```
./gradlew bootRun
```

If you started the backend on a port other than 8080, tell the frontend:

```
./gradlew bootRun --args='--app.backend.base-url=http://localhost:8090/v1'
```

**The first start takes several minutes.** Vaadin downloads Node.js and the npm
packages it needs to build the browser bundle. This happens once; later starts
take seconds. Wait for:

```
Started FrontendApplication
```

### Step 4 — Open it

Go to **http://localhost:8081** in a browser.

You will see the Dashboard, showing the demo user's portfolio.

### Step 5 — Load live market data

The demo data has transactions but no prices yet, so the value columns start
empty. Click **"Refresh market data"** on the dashboard.

This calls CoinGecko and NBP and fills in every figure: current prices, market
value, profit and loss, and the total in złoty. From then on the backend refreshes
by itself every fifteen minutes for as long as it is running.

### Step 6 — Try it out

- **Dashboard** — switch between the two demo portfolios; note that "Long term"
  reports in złoty and "Experiments" in dollars.
- **Transactions** — add a purchase and watch the dashboard totals change. Try
  selling more than you own; the application refuses and explains why.
- **Alerts** — add a price alert with a very low threshold so it is certain to
  fire, then press "Check alerts now". It appears on the dashboard.
- **Assets** — add another coin using its CoinGecko identifier, for example
  `dogecoin` or `litecoin`, then refresh market data to price it.
- **Market data** — see exactly what came from each of the two providers.

### Stopping

Press `Ctrl+C` in each terminal. The backend keeps its data in a `data/` folder,
so it is all still there next time. Deleting that folder resets everything and
re-seeds the demo data.

### Running the tests

In the backend folder:

```
./gradlew test
```

286 tests. The coverage report lands in
`build/reports/jacoco/test/html/index.html`. Running `./gradlew check` also fails
the build if coverage falls below 65%.

---

## 3. Technical notes

### External API access

**Neither external API needs a key, an account, or any authorisation.** Both are
public and free. There is nothing to configure before running the project — this
is why they were chosen over alternatives that require registration.

| Provider | What it supplies | Endpoint used |
|---|---|---|
| **CoinGecko** | Cryptocurrency prices in USD, plus 24-hour change | `https://api.coingecko.com/api/v3/simple/price` |
| **NBP** (Polish National Bank) | Daily average exchange rate table | `https://api.nbp.pl/api/exchangerates/tables/A` |

You can confirm both are reachable from your machine without running anything:

```
curl "https://api.coingecko.com/api/v3/simple/price?ids=bitcoin&vs_currencies=usd"
```

```
curl "https://api.nbp.pl/api/exchangerates/tables/A?format=json"
```

**Rate limits.** CoinGecko's free tier is rate limited. The default refresh
interval of fifteen minutes stays comfortably inside it. If you shorten
`app.scheduler.price-refresh-ms` a lot, you may be throttled; the application
reports that as HTTP 503 rather than pretending the data is missing.

**When a provider is down.** The two are refreshed independently. If CoinGecko is
unreachable, exchange rates are still updated, and the response says which
provider failed. Nothing is silently skipped.

### The API itself

The REST API has **48 endpoints** under `/v1`, covering GET, POST, PUT and DELETE.
The full list is in `README.md`.

Errors always come back in the same shape, so a client only needs to handle one:

```json
{
  "status": 422,
  "error": "Unprocessable Entity",
  "message": "Cannot sell 99 BTC: portfolio only holds 0.35000000",
  "fieldErrors": {},
  "timestamp": "2026-07-30T08:34:59.645"
}
```

| Status | When |
|---|---|
| 400 | Malformed body, failed validation, missing or mistyped parameter |
| 404 | No such record |
| 409 | Would break a uniqueness rule, e.g. a username already taken |
| 422 | Valid request that breaks a domain rule, e.g. overselling |
| 503 | An external provider is unavailable |

For validation failures, `fieldErrors` names each offending field:

```json
{
  "status": 400,
  "error": "Bad Request",
  "message": "Request validation failed",
  "fieldErrors": {
    "username": "username must be 3-60 characters",
    "email": "email must be a valid address"
  }
}
```

### Configuration

Any property can be overridden on the command line as `--name=value`. The ones
that matter most:

| Property | Default | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `server.port` | `8080` (backend), `8081` (frontend) | HTTP port |
| `app.backend.base-url` | `http://localhost:8080/v1` | Frontend: where the API is |
| `app.cors.allowed-origins` | `http://localhost:8081` | Backend: where the UI is |
| `app.scheduler.enabled` | `true` | Turn scheduled jobs off entirely |
| `app.scheduler.price-refresh-ms` | `900000` | Price refresh interval |
| `app.alerts.cooldown-minutes` | `60` | Minimum gap before an alert refires |
| `app.seed-demo-data` | `true` | Seed demo data into an empty database |

If you move the backend to a different port, change **both**
`app.backend.base-url` on the frontend and `app.cors.allowed-origins` on the
backend, or the browser will block the calls.

### Using MySQL instead of H2

H2 is the default so that the project runs with no setup at all. To use MySQL:

```sql
CREATE DATABASE portfolio_tracker CHARACTER SET utf8mb4 COLLATE utf8mb4_unicode_ci;
```

```
./gradlew bootRun --args='--spring.profiles.active=mysql --spring.datasource.username=root --spring.datasource.password=YOUR_PASSWORD'
```

Credentials are passed on the command line rather than committed to the
repository. The schema is created automatically on first start.

### Authentication

There is none, and none is required by the project brief. The frontend shows the
data of one user, chosen by `app.backend.user-id` (the seeded demo user by
default). The `users` table and the audit trail are structured so that
authentication could be added without reshaping the data model.

### Requirements checklist

| Requirement | Where it is met |
|---|---|
| 20+ REST endpoints, all four HTTP methods | 48 endpoints; see `README.md` |
| 2+ external data sources | CoinGecko and NBP, combined to value a portfolio |
| Scheduler | `MarketDataScheduler`: price/rate refresh and alert evaluation, plus nightly history purge |
| 10+ database write operations | Over 20; CRUD for five entities, plus snapshots, rates, alert events and audit rows |
| 65%+ test coverage | 286 tests, 91.9% instruction coverage, enforced by the build |
| 2+ design patterns | Strategy, Factory, Observer, Adapter and Facade |
| Vaadin view layer | Separate frontend repository, five screens |
